Subject: [PATCH 0/1] PCI: work around ULi EHCI MSI data corruption

This independent PCI quirk applies to vanilla v7.3-rc1. It has no SPARC
or M3000 patch dependency and is intended for PCI review, with USB and
SPARC reviewers copied. It matches ULi 10b9:5239 revision 01, subsystem
10b9:5238, and makes the USB HCD use INTx instead of MSI.

On an Ultra45, programming the EHCI MSI data field by word or dword writes
replicated the low byte into the high byte. The adjacent OHCI function
preserved the same word-write values. The commit describes this observed
behavior without claiming that all architectures or revisions are affected.
The subsystem match limits scope to tested hardware, not a proven causal
relationship with that subsystem ID. Detailed readbacks are below the
patch separator for PCI review.

The integrated test kernel selected INTx automatically. The onboard hub
and a SanDisk USB storage device enumerated at 480Mbps. Three controller
rebinds, repeated control requests and 640MiB of direct reads passed,
with matching repeated SHA256 results and no new kernel log messages.
No USB filesystem was mounted or written. OHCI and NICs retained MSI;
network transfers also passed checksum checks. Kernel taint stayed zero.

Runtime coverage is one Ultra45 with the generic sparc64 fixes and M3000
patches also applied. A prior pre-follow-up baseline passed a temporary
INTx test. A quirk-only vanilla image has not booted. Other architectures,
USB writes and prolonged USB stress remain untested. No hardware erratum
has been identified, and a cross-architecture silicon cause is unproven.

The standalone patch passed full sparc64 UP and SMP W=1 vmlinux builds
on the vanilla base and independent git-am verification. This does not
constitute an x86 build or cross-architecture runtime test.

Codex assisted with implementation, source review and test preparation.

